The institution's mission is to "Preserve the Pantanal" and the institution's main objectives and lines of action according to the Articles of Association are: 
promote, support, publicize, coordinate, develop, participate in and execute programs, plans, projects and activities related to education, culture, history, tradition, socio-economics, sports, tourism, conservation and preservation of the environment, projects and activities related to culture, tradition and improvement of environmental quality, especially in the Pantanal Biome; 
promote, support, disseminate, coordinate, develop, participate in and execute programs, plans, projects and activities related to education, training and qualification of human resources in the areas of culture, history, tradition, socio-economics, sports, tourism, conservation and preservation of the environment; 
promote, support, disseminate, coordinate, develop and execute studies and research in the cultural, historical, environmental and socio-economic areas;
promote, carry out and disseminate research and studies, organize documentation and develop projects in the areas of culture and the environment; 
provide and perform services, advisory services and consultancy to governmental and non-governmental institutions, public and private companies, individuals and legal entities, in the areas of planning and execution of activities related to education, culture, history, tradition, socio-economics, sports, tourism, conservation, environmental preservation, environmental services and improvement of environmental quality; 
promote, organize, produce, publicize and participate in national and international events and campaigns to support the conservation of environmental heritage, improvement of environmental quality, culture, tradition, history, sports and tourism; 
defend, in court and out of court, the diffuse, collective and homogeneous individual rights and interests of the needy communities to which it provides assistance and of the environment.

evaluate-notes (direct extract):
In 2023, the main highlights of SPVS's performance were:
• Management and handling of three of our own Natural Reserves, totaling more than 19 thousand hectares
• Generation of more than 6.2 million reais in Ecological ICMS, for the municipalities of Antonina and Guaraqueçaba (PR); 
Development of Agroforestry Systems (SAFs) methodologies
with training of local communities;
• Conservation, management and research with endemic species, threatened with extinction, such as the purple-faced parrot, the red-breasted parrot, -purple and black-faced lion tamarin;
• Generation of employment and income in the region, contributing to the improvement of socioeconomic indicators
• Development of public use and ecotourism activities in Natural Reserves; Carrying out nature conservation education and communication
activities with a wide range of audiences;
• Support for the creation of the Payment for Environmental Services Law (PSA) in the municipality of Antonina (PR);
• Development of advocacy initiatives and partnerships with the Public Authorities to strengthen nature conservation actions and policies;
Participation in regional environmental meetings, committees and forums Formalization of partnerships with the private sector for the development of projects; 
Use of media, social networks and events to publicize initiatives
and raise awareness in society about environmental issues

define-notes (direct extract):
Its mission is to promote nature conservation through rewilding measures in Portugal.
Rewilding Vision
In the long-term, we want the Greater Côa Valley to be a wilder place, with fully functioning coherent ecosystems and abundant wildlife. A place where natural processes and complete trophic chains play key roles in the regeneration of the landscape.
Already, wildlife has returned to parts of the rewilding area, with species such as the Iberian wolf, red deer and roe deer expanding their ranges. We also want to build a nature and culture-based economy to create communal support for a wilder environment in which natural processes and key wildlife shape the landscape.

Project Objectives
The main aim of this project is to develop a coherent ecosystem and strengthen the wildlife corridor of the Côa Valley (120.000 hectares). In addition, the project aims to build a nature and culture-based economy to create communal support for a wilder environment in which natural processes and key wildlife shape the landscape. More specifically, the project aims to:
Increase the area of land under restoration in order to increase connectivity in the Côa Valley;
Bring back wildlife, and improve the status of prey base for Iberian lynx, wolf, Bonelli´s eagle, Iberian imperial eagle and black vulture;
Reduce the main threats to habitats and species (poaching, poisoning and fire), wolf-husbandry conflict and the use of toxic ammunition;
Create a scalable nature-based economy that improves the socio-economic situation of local communities;
Increase awareness about nature conservation, and create pride towards Rewilded Landscapes at local and national level.
